From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: abhay-byte <abhay02delhi@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 20:00:00 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] pan/bi: build LCRA constraints once for both register file sizes, with global-only liveness and a sorted queue biblioklept

Building the LCRA constraints dominated register allocation time on big
DXVK shaders. Three changes, none of which changes the output:

- Block liveness only tracks "global" nodes, the ones some block reads
  before writing. Only those can be live at a block boundary, so
  live_in/live_out shrink from every node to that subset, and
  bi_lower_vector's cleanup walks only them too.

- The interference graph is the same for the 32 register (split file)
  and the 64 register attempt; only affinities differ. Build it once
  with both affinity masks and the forced registers, and solve each
  attempt from that, instead of rebuilding liveness and interference
  for the second attempt.

- Constraints are queued while building and sorted into the rows at the
  end (counting sort by key, then stably by row), instead of inserting
  each into a sorted sparse row, which memmoved ~56 entries per insert.
  The queue is flushed every 1M entries to bound memory. Rows end up
  with the same contents and the same sparse/dense choice.

Output is identical (same binary) on all 67539 shaders of the replay
corpus. x86 host, 18 synthetic DXVK-GPL shaders in the range of the
slow NFS Most Wanted pipelines: 967 -> 472 ms total, RA 843 -> 348 ms.
---
